  "notes": "Has carried the scheme since 2005 (repainted from Canyon Blue to Heart-era tail). Drawn from 2026 photos of N214WN, both from the left and below (levelled; the aft end is foreshortened). The cross bottony's trefoil ends are drawn as rounded lobes. Both 2026 photos show the left engine unpainted silver-white and the right engine red. The right side is not seen. Re-checked 2026-11-15: the fin is the state jets' Heart fin as re-measured on Colorado One (the same fin on every 737), copied with its 'Southwest' title and flag. 'Maryland One' measured on the January 2026 left photo against the nose and the forward door (1.4-2.4 m back: further forward than on the other state jets, on the red), two weights, a pair of titles per side (the right mirrored: both photos are of the left side), on the red, lower than on the other state jets (y -0.21, as first drawn). The cross bottony's round ends made smooth, the corners where they meet the arms kept sharp. Drawn on the 737-700. Nose re-checked 2026-11-27 (N214WN side-on and from the left, October 2025 and January 2026): the red quarter's top edge runs from under the forward door nearly level, just under the windscreen, to the nose tip, so the lower nose and chin are red; it had been a diagonal from the cockpit down to the chin, leaving the nose white with red running down beside it from ahead. Both sides checked separately 2026-11-27 on the owner's review: the right side (LAX 2011-10-11 climbing out, Orlando 2016 from ahead, the 2021 ramp photo) carries the Crossland half counterchanged, red where the left is white and white where it's red: the upper forward fuselage and the crown over the cockpit red, the lower nose and chin white, the flag's arms swapped too. So the chin is red on the left and white on the right, meeting on the belly's centerline, and the crown over the cockpit is white on the left, red on the right. Drawn as left and right areas with the same outlines. The right side's 'Maryland One' is red on the white lower nose (LAX 2011-10-11), white on the red on the left."
}
